window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-102"]=[{"id":"K102.01","q":"102:1–8","s":102,"surah":"al-Takāthur","surah_ar":"التكاثر","title":"al-Takāthur · Heading and verses","cairo":"K IV:791–792 · T XVI:558–564","source_page_first":791,"source_page_last":792,"chunk":"passages-102","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Heading and verses","html":"<p>Meccan; eight verses. [K-DKA adds: revealed after al-Kawthar <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(الكوثر)</bdi>.]\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n“Rivalry in increase has distracted you, until you visited the graves. No indeed! You will come to know. Again, no indeed! You will come to know. No indeed! If only you knew with knowledge of certainty! You will surely see the blazing Fire. Then you will surely see it with the very certainty of sight. Then that day you will surely be questioned about the pleasures you enjoyed.”</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K102.01"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"مكية، وآياتها 8 «نزلت بعد الكوثر» بسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\nألهاكم التكاثر (1) حتى زرتم المقابر (2) كلا سوف تعلمون (3) ثم كلا سوف تعلمون (4)\nكلا لو تعلمون علم اليقين (5) لترون الجحيم (6) ثم لترونها عين اليقين (7) ثم لتسئلن يومئذ عن النعيم (8)","layers":[{"id":"K102.01","heading":"K102.01 — Heading and verses","text":"Meccan; eight verses. [K-DKA adds: revealed after al-Kawthar.]\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n“Rivalry in increase has distracted you, until you visited the graves. No indeed! You will come to know. Again, no indeed! You will come to know. No indeed! If only you knew with knowledge of certainty! You will surely see the blazing Fire. Then you will surely see it with the very certainty of sight. Then that day you will surely be questioned about the pleasures you enjoyed.”","title":"Heading and verses","author":"kashshaf","surah":102,"ar":"مكية، وآياتها 8 «نزلت بعد الكوثر» بسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nألهاكم التكاثر (1) حتى زرتم المقابر (2) كلا سوف تعلمون (3) ثم كلا سوف تعلمون (4)\n\nكلا لو تعلمون علم اليقين (5) لترون الجحيم (6) ثم لترونها عين اليقين (7) ثم لتسئلن يومئذ عن النعيم (8)","html":"<p>Meccan; eight verses. [K-DKA adds: revealed after al-Kawthar <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(الكوثر)</bdi>.]\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n“Rivalry in increase has distracted you, until you visited the graves. No indeed! You will come to know. Again, no indeed! You will come to know. No indeed! If only you knew with knowledge of certainty! You will surely see the blazing Fire. Then you will surely see it with the very certainty of sight. Then that day you will surely be questioned about the pleasures you enjoyed.”</p>","lemma":""}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"102:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ أَلْهَىٰكُمُ ٱلتَّكَاثُرُ","en":"Rivalry in worldly increase distracteth you"},{"key":"102:2","number":2,"ar":"حَتَّىٰ زُرْتُمُ ٱلْمَقَابِرَ","en":"Until ye come to the graves"},{"key":"102:3","number":3,"ar":"كَلَّا سَوْفَ تَعْلَمُونَ","en":"Nay, but ye will come to know"},{"key":"102:4","number":4,"ar":"ثُمَّ كَلَّا سَوْفَ تَعْلَمُونَ","en":"Nay, but ye will come to know"},{"key":"102:5","number":5,"ar":"كَلَّا لَوْ تَعْلَمُونَ عِلْمَ ٱلْيَقِينِ","en":"Nay, would that ye knew (now) with a sure knowledge"},{"key":"102:6","number":6,"ar":"لَتَرَوُنَّ ٱلْجَحِيمَ","en":"For ye will behold hell-fire"},{"key":"102:7","number":7,"ar":"ثُمَّ لَتَرَوُنَّهَا عَيْنَ ٱلْيَقِينِ","en":"Aye, ye will behold it with sure vision"},{"key":"102:8","number":8,"ar":"ثُمَّ لَتُسْـَٔلُنَّ يَوْمَئِذٍ عَنِ ٱلنَّعِيمِ","en":"Then, on that day, ye will be asked concerning pleasure"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ أَلْهَىٰكُمُ ٱلتَّكَاثُرُ ﴿1﴾ حَتَّىٰ زُرْتُمُ ٱلْمَقَابِرَ ﴿2﴾ كَلَّا سَوْفَ تَعْلَمُونَ ﴿3﴾ ثُمَّ كَلَّا سَوْفَ تَعْلَمُونَ ﴿4﴾ كَلَّا لَوْ تَعْلَمُونَ عِلْمَ ٱلْيَقِينِ ﴿5﴾ لَتَرَوُنَّ ٱلْجَحِيمَ ﴿6﴾ ثُمَّ لَتَرَوُنَّهَا عَيْنَ ٱلْيَقِينِ ﴿7﴾ ثُمَّ لَتُسْـَٔلُنَّ يَوْمَئِذٍ عَنِ ٱلنَّعِيمِ ﴿8﴾","quran_en":"1. Rivalry in worldly increase distracteth you 2. Until ye come to the graves 3. Nay, but ye will come to know 4. Nay, but ye will come to know 5. Nay, would that ye knew (now) with a sure knowledge 6. For ye will behold hell-fire 7. Aye, ye will behold it with sure vision 8. Then, on that day, ye will be asked concerning pleasure"},{"id":"K102.02","q":"102:1–2","s":102,"surah":"al-Takāthur","surah_ar":"التكاثر","title":"al-Takāthur · Rivalry and the graves","cairo":"K IV:791–792 · T XVI:558–564","source_page_first":791,"source_page_last":792,"chunk":"passages-102","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Rivalry and the graves","html":"<p>Alhāhu <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ألهاه)</bdi> ʿan <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عن)</bdi> kadhā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كذا)</bdi> and aqhāhu <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(أقهاه)</bdi> mean that something occupied him and distracted him from something else. Takāthur <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(تكاثر)</bdi> is competing in abundance and boasting of it: one group says, “We are more numerous,” and the other says, “We are more numerous.” It is reported that Banū ʿAbd Manāf and Banū Sahm boasted to one another about which was greater in number. Banū ʿAbd Manāf outnumbered them. Banū Sahm said: “Aggression destroyed us in the age of ignorance; count against us both the living and the dead.” Banū Sahm then outnumbered them.\nThe meaning is: you competed over the number of the living, and once you exhausted their number, you turned to the graves and competed over the dead. Their proceeding to mention the dead is expressed as visiting the graves, in mockery of them. It is also said that they actually visited the graves, saying, “This is so-and-so&#x27;s grave; this is so-and-so&#x27;s grave,” while boasting to one another.\nThe meaning is that this distracted you—something that does not concern you and yields you no benefit in this world or the next—from what does concern you: the affairs of religion, which are more important and more deserving of attention than anything else.\nOr He means: rivalry in wealth and children distracted you until you died and were buried. You spent your lives seeking this world, racing toward it and throwing yourselves upon it, until death came while you cared for nothing else. It distracted you from what had a greater claim upon you: striving for your final outcome and working for your hereafter. Visiting the graves is an expression for death. A poet said:\n“This year no intimate who has tasted a shared attachment\nwill secure ten [nights of exclusive companionship], unless he visits the grave.”\nAnother said:\n“Abū Mālik <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(مَالِك)</bdi> visited the graves,\nand became the meanest of their visitors.”\nIbn ʿAbbās read a-alhākum <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(أألهاكم)</bdi>, as a question whose purpose is to elicit acknowledgment.</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K102.02"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Outnumbering; the irony of visiting graves (XVI, 558–559)","html":"<p>On “Banū Sahm outnumbered them”: they defeated them through greater numbers, from the expression kāthartuhu fa-kathartuhu <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كاثرته فكثرته)</bdi>, “I competed with him in numbers and outnumbered him.” Takāthur <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(تكاثر)</bdi> is striving to increase one&#x27;s wealth and numbers.\nOn “you turned to the graves and competed over the dead”: according to this, “the graves” is an indirect expression for moving from mentioning the living to mentioning the dead in mutual boasting. It is mockery because visiting graves was prescribed to recall death, reject love of this world, and abandon ostentation and boasting. These people reversed this: they made visiting graves a cause of greater hardness of heart, immersion in worldly love, and boasting of greater numbers.\nWe have transmitted from Muslim, Abū Dāwūd, and al-Nasāʾī, from Burayda, that God&#x27;s Messenger said: “I forbade you to visit graves; now visit them.” Abū Dāwūd&#x27;s version reads: “Visit them, for they remind you of the hereafter.”\nOn “or He means: rivalry in wealth and children distracted you until you died”: the three interpretations thus reduce to ‘visiting’ meaning either the transition from mentioning one group to mentioning another, actual visiting, or death. “Spending” is a circumstantial expression attached to “distracted you”; “from what had a greater claim upon you” is also governed by “distracted you.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T102.01"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The verse about a shared attachment (559–560)","html":"<p>On “This year no intimate…”: al-Fāʾiq says: “A woman&#x27;s ḍamd <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضمد)</bdi> is her joining together and taking two lovers.” Abū Dhuʾayb said:\n“You wish to join me and Khālid as your lovers—\ncan two swords, woe to you, be joined in one sheath?”\nThe author of the quoted verse is Miqdād ibn Ḥassān al-Zubayrī. It is preceded by:\n“I have found a shared attachment a grievous thing.”\nIn the age of ignorance a woman would take a lover besides her husband; that is ḍamd <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضمد)</bdi>.\nOn “ten” (ʿashran <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عَشْرًا)</bdi>): ten nights. A reading with kasra <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كَسْرَة (ـِ))</bdi> on the ʿayn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عين (ع))</bdi>, ʿishran <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عِشْرًا)</bdi>, is also transmitted, meaning companionship. Muʿāshara <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(معاشرة)</bdi> means association, as does taʿāshur <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(تعاشر)</bdi>; the corresponding noun is ʿishra <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عِشْرة)</bdi>. Khalīl <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(خليل)</bdi> means husband. The meaning is that a husband will not secure exclusive companionship with a woman for ten nights unless he dies. “Who has tasted a shared attachment” describes the intimate. [The compressed verse and its explanation remain difficult; the alternatives are retained rather than conflated.]</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T102.02"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"ألهاه عن كذا وأقهاه: إذا شغله . والتكاثر التبارى في الكثرة والتباهي بها، وأن يقول هؤلاء: نحن أكثر، وهؤلاء: نحن أكثر. روى أن بنى عبد مناف وبنى سهم تفاخروا أيهم أكثر عددا، فكثرهم بنو عبد مناف فقالت بنوسهم: إن البغي أهلكنا في الجاهلية فعادونا بالأحياء والأموات، فكثرتهم بنوسهم. والمعنى: أنكم تكاثرتم بالأحياء حتى إذا استوعبتم عددهم صرتم إلى المقابر فتكاثرتم بالأموات: عبر عن بلوغهم ذكر الموتى بزيارة المقابر تهكما بهم: وقيل كانوا يزورون المقابر فيقولون: هذا قبر فلان وهذا قبر فلان عند تفاخرهم.\nوالمعنى: ألهاكم ذلك- وهو مما لا يعنيكم ولا يجدى عليكم في دنياكم وآخرتكم- عما يعنيكم من أمر الدين الذي هو أهم وأعنى من كل مهم. أو أراد ألهاكم التكاثر بالأموال والأولاد إلى أن \nمتم وقبرتم، منفقين أعماركم في طلب الدنيا والاستباق إليها والتهالك عليها، إلى أن أتاكم الموت لا هم لكم غيرها، عما هو أولى بكم من السعى لعاقبتكم والعمل لآخرتكم. وزيارة القبور:\nعبارة عن الموت. قال:\nلن يخلص العام خليل عشرا ... ذاق الضماد أو يزور القبرا \nوقال: زار القبور أبو مالك ... فأصبح الأم زوارها»\nوقرأ ابن عباس: أألهاكم؟ على الاستفهام الذي معناه التقرير\nمكية، وهي ثماني آيات\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\nقوله: (فكثرتهم بنوسهم)، أي: غلبوهم بالكثرة، من قولهم: كاثرته فكثرته. والتكاثر تكلف الكثرة مالا وعددا.\nقوله: (صرتم إلى المقابر فتكاثرتم بالأموات)، فعلى هذا، {المقابر} كناية عن الانتقال من ذكر الأحياء إلى ذكر الأموات تفاخرا؛ وإنما كان تهكما، لأن زيارة القبور شرعت لتذكر الموت، ورفض حب الدنيا، وترك المباهاة والتفاخر. وهؤلاء عكسوا، جيث جعلوا زيارة القبور سببا لمزيد القسوة، والاستغراق في حب الدنيا، والتفاخر في الكثرة. روينا عن مسلم وأبي داود والنسائي، عن بريدة قال: قال رسول الله? : \"نهيتكم عن زيارة القبور؛ فزوروها\". وفي رواية أبي داود: \"فزوروها؛ فإنها تذكركم الآخرة\".\nقوله: (أو أراد: ألهاكم التكاثر بالأموال والأولاد إلى أن متم)، فحاصل الوجوه الثلاثة راجع إلى أن المراد بالزيارة، إما الانتقال من الذكر إلى الذكر، أو إلى حقيقة الزيارة، أو إلى الموت. و\"منفقين\" حال من {ألهاكم}، و\"عما هو أولى بكم\" متعلق بألهاكم.\nقوله: (لن يخلص العام)، البيت قال في \"الفائق\": \"ضمد المرأة جمعها واتخاذها\nالخليلين\"، قال أبو ذؤيب:\nتريدين كيما تضمديني وخالدا وهل يجمع السيفات ويحك في غمد\nقائله: مقداد بن حسان الزبيري، قبله:\nإني رأيت الضمد شيئا نكرا\nوكانت المرأة في الجاهلية تتخذ سوى زوجها خليلا، وهو الضمد.\nقوله: (عشرا)، أي: عشر ليال، وروي بكسر العين، أي: معاشرة، والمعاشرة: المخالطة، وكذلك التعاشر، والاسم: العشرة. والخليل: الزوج. المعنى: لن يخلص زوج معاشرة امرأة عشر ليال، إلا أن يموت. ذاق الضماد: صفة الخليل.","layers":[{"id":"K102.02","heading":"K102.02 — Rivalry and the graves","text":"Alhāhu ʿan kadhā and aqhāhu mean that something occupied him and distracted him from something else. Takāthur is competing in abundance and boasting of it: one group says, “We are more numerous,” and the other says, “We are more numerous.” It is reported that Banū ʿAbd Manāf and Banū Sahm boasted to one another about which was greater in number. Banū ʿAbd Manāf outnumbered them. Banū Sahm said: “Aggression destroyed us in the age of ignorance; count against us both the living and the dead.” Banū Sahm then outnumbered them.\nThe meaning is: you competed over the number of the living, and once you exhausted their number, you turned to the graves and competed over the dead. Their proceeding to mention the dead is expressed as visiting the graves, in mockery of them. It is also said that they actually visited the graves, saying, “This is so-and-so's grave; this is so-and-so's grave,” while boasting to one another.\nThe meaning is that this distracted you—something that does not concern you and yields you no benefit in this world or the next—from what does concern you: the affairs of religion, which are more important and more deserving of attention than anything else.\nOr He means: rivalry in wealth and children distracted you until you died and were buried. You spent your lives seeking this world, racing toward it and throwing yourselves upon it, until death came while you cared for nothing else. It distracted you from what had a greater claim upon you: striving for your final outcome and working for your hereafter. Visiting the graves is an expression for death. A poet said:\n“This year no intimate who has tasted a shared attachment\nwill secure ten [nights of exclusive companionship], unless he visits the grave.”\nAnother said:\n“Abū Mālik visited the graves,\nand became the meanest of their visitors.”\nIbn ʿAbbās read a-alhākum, as a question whose purpose is to elicit acknowledgment.","title":"Rivalry and the graves","author":"kashshaf","surah":102,"ar":"ألهاه عن كذا وأقهاه: إذا شغله . والتكاثر التبارى في الكثرة والتباهي بها، وأن يقول هؤلاء: نحن أكثر، وهؤلاء: نحن أكثر. روى أن بنى عبد مناف وبنى سهم تفاخروا أيهم أكثر عددا، فكثرهم بنو عبد مناف فقالت بنوسهم: إن البغي أهلكنا في الجاهلية فعادونا بالأحياء والأموات، فكثرتهم بنوسهم. والمعنى: أنكم تكاثرتم بالأحياء حتى إذا استوعبتم عددهم صرتم إلى المقابر فتكاثرتم بالأموات: عبر عن بلوغهم ذكر الموتى بزيارة المقابر تهكما بهم: وقيل كانوا يزورون المقابر فيقولون: هذا قبر فلان وهذا قبر فلان عند تفاخرهم.\n\nوالمعنى: ألهاكم ذلك- وهو مما لا يعنيكم ولا يجدى عليكم في دنياكم وآخرتكم- عما يعنيكم من أمر الدين الذي هو أهم وأعنى من كل مهم. أو أراد ألهاكم التكاثر بالأموال والأولاد إلى أن \n\nمتم وقبرتم، منفقين أعماركم في طلب الدنيا والاستباق إليها والتهالك عليها، إلى أن أتاكم الموت لا هم لكم غيرها، عما هو أولى بكم من السعى لعاقبتكم والعمل لآخرتكم. وزيارة القبور:\n\nعبارة عن الموت. قال:\n\nلن يخلص العام خليل عشرا ... ذاق الضماد أو يزور القبرا \n\nوقال: زار القبور أبو مالك ... فأصبح الأم زوارها»\n\nوقرأ ابن عباس: أألهاكم؟ على الاستفهام الذي معناه التقرير","html":"<p>Alhāhu <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ألهاه)</bdi> ʿan <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عن)</bdi> kadhā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كذا)</bdi> and aqhāhu <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(أقهاه)</bdi> mean that something occupied him and distracted him from something else. Takāthur <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(تكاثر)</bdi> is competing in abundance and boasting of it: one group says, “We are more numerous,” and the other says, “We are more numerous.” It is reported that Banū ʿAbd Manāf and Banū Sahm boasted to one another about which was greater in number. Banū ʿAbd Manāf outnumbered them. Banū Sahm said: “Aggression destroyed us in the age of ignorance; count against us both the living and the dead.” Banū Sahm then outnumbered them.\nThe meaning is: you competed over the number of the living, and once you exhausted their number, you turned to the graves and competed over the dead. Their proceeding to mention the dead is expressed as visiting the graves, in mockery of them. It is also said that they actually visited the graves, saying, “This is so-and-so&#x27;s grave; this is so-and-so&#x27;s grave,” while boasting to one another.\nThe meaning is that this distracted you—something that does not concern you and yields you no benefit in this world or the next—from what does concern you: the affairs of religion, which are more important and more deserving of attention than anything else.\nOr He means: rivalry in wealth and children distracted you until you died and were buried. You spent your lives seeking this world, racing toward it and throwing yourselves upon it, until death came while you cared for nothing else. It distracted you from what had a greater claim upon you: striving for your final outcome and working for your hereafter. Visiting the graves is an expression for death. A poet said:\n“This year no intimate who has tasted a shared attachment\nwill secure ten [nights of exclusive companionship], unless he visits the grave.”\nAnother said:\n“Abū Mālik <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(مَالِك)</bdi> visited the graves,\nand became the meanest of their visitors.”\nIbn ʿAbbās read a-alhākum <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(أألهاكم)</bdi>, as a question whose purpose is to elicit acknowledgment.</p>","lemma":""},{"id":"T102.01","heading":"T102.01 — Outnumbering; the irony of visiting graves (XVI, 558–559)","text":"On “Banū Sahm outnumbered them”: they defeated them through greater numbers, from the expression kāthartuhu fa-kathartuhu, “I competed with him in numbers and outnumbered him.” Takāthur is striving to increase one's wealth and numbers.\nOn “you turned to the graves and competed over the dead”: according to this, “the graves” is an indirect expression for moving from mentioning the living to mentioning the dead in mutual boasting. It is mockery because visiting graves was prescribed to recall death, reject love of this world, and abandon ostentation and boasting. These people reversed this: they made visiting graves a cause of greater hardness of heart, immersion in worldly love, and boasting of greater numbers.\nWe have transmitted from Muslim, Abū Dāwūd, and al-Nasāʾī, from Burayda, that God's Messenger said: “I forbade you to visit graves; now visit them.” Abū Dāwūd's version reads: “Visit them, for they remind you of the hereafter.”\nOn “or He means: rivalry in wealth and children distracted you until you died”: the three interpretations thus reduce to ‘visiting’ meaning either the transition from mentioning one group to mentioning another, actual visiting, or death. “Spending” is a circumstantial expression attached to “distracted you”; “from what had a greater claim upon you” is also governed by “distracted you.”","title":"Outnumbering; the irony of visiting graves (XVI, 558–559)","author":"tibi","on":["K102.02"],"anchor":"K102.02","surah":102,"ar":"مكية، وهي ثماني آيات\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nقوله: (فكثرتهم بنوسهم)، أي: غلبوهم بالكثرة، من قولهم: كاثرته فكثرته. والتكاثر تكلف الكثرة مالا وعددا.\n\nقوله: (صرتم إلى المقابر فتكاثرتم بالأموات)، فعلى هذا، {المقابر} كناية عن الانتقال من ذكر الأحياء إلى ذكر الأموات تفاخرا؛ وإنما كان تهكما، لأن زيارة القبور شرعت لتذكر الموت، ورفض حب الدنيا، وترك المباهاة والتفاخر. وهؤلاء عكسوا، جيث جعلوا زيارة القبور سببا لمزيد القسوة، والاستغراق في حب الدنيا، والتفاخر في الكثرة. روينا عن مسلم وأبي داود والنسائي، عن بريدة قال: قال رسول الله? : \"نهيتكم عن زيارة القبور؛ فزوروها\". وفي رواية أبي داود: \"فزوروها؛ فإنها تذكركم الآخرة\".\n\nقوله: (أو أراد: ألهاكم التكاثر بالأموال والأولاد إلى أن متم)، فحاصل الوجوه الثلاثة راجع إلى أن المراد بالزيارة، إما الانتقال من الذكر إلى الذكر، أو إلى حقيقة الزيارة، أو إلى الموت. و\"منفقين\" حال من {ألهاكم}، و\"عما هو أولى بكم\" متعلق بألهاكم.","html":"<p>On “Banū Sahm outnumbered them”: they defeated them through greater numbers, from the expression kāthartuhu fa-kathartuhu <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كاثرته فكثرته)</bdi>, “I competed with him in numbers and outnumbered him.” Takāthur <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(تكاثر)</bdi> is striving to increase one&#x27;s wealth and numbers.\nOn “you turned to the graves and competed over the dead”: according to this, “the graves” is an indirect expression for moving from mentioning the living to mentioning the dead in mutual boasting. It is mockery because visiting graves was prescribed to recall death, reject love of this world, and abandon ostentation and boasting. These people reversed this: they made visiting graves a cause of greater hardness of heart, immersion in worldly love, and boasting of greater numbers.\nWe have transmitted from Muslim, Abū Dāwūd, and al-Nasāʾī, from Burayda, that God&#x27;s Messenger said: “I forbade you to visit graves; now visit them.” Abū Dāwūd&#x27;s version reads: “Visit them, for they remind you of the hereafter.”\nOn “or He means: rivalry in wealth and children distracted you until you died”: the three interpretations thus reduce to ‘visiting’ meaning either the transition from mentioning one group to mentioning another, actual visiting, or death. “Spending” is a circumstantial expression attached to “distracted you”; “from what had a greater claim upon you” is also governed by “distracted you.”</p>","lemma":"فكثرتهم بنوسهم"},{"id":"T102.02","heading":"T102.02 — The verse about a shared attachment (559–560)","text":"On “This year no intimate…”: al-Fāʾiq says: “A woman's ḍamd is her joining together and taking two lovers.” Abū Dhuʾayb said:\n“You wish to join me and Khālid as your lovers—\ncan two swords, woe to you, be joined in one sheath?”\nThe author of the quoted verse is Miqdād ibn Ḥassān al-Zubayrī. It is preceded by:\n“I have found a shared attachment a grievous thing.”\nIn the age of ignorance a woman would take a lover besides her husband; that is ḍamd.\nOn “ten” (ʿashran): ten nights. A reading with kasra on the ʿayn, ʿishran, is also transmitted, meaning companionship. Muʿāshara means association, as does taʿāshur; the corresponding noun is ʿishra. Khalīl means husband. The meaning is that a husband will not secure exclusive companionship with a woman for ten nights unless he dies. “Who has tasted a shared attachment” describes the intimate. [The compressed verse and its explanation remain difficult; the alternatives are retained rather than conflated.]","title":"The verse about a shared attachment (559–560)","author":"tibi","on":["K102.02"],"anchor":"K102.02","surah":102,"ar":"قوله: (لن يخلص العام)، البيت قال في \"الفائق\": \"ضمد المرأة جمعها واتخاذها\n\nالخليلين\"، قال أبو ذؤيب:\n\nتريدين كيما تضمديني وخالدا وهل يجمع السيفات ويحك في غمد\n\nقائله: مقداد بن حسان الزبيري، قبله:\n\nإني رأيت الضمد شيئا نكرا\n\nوكانت المرأة في الجاهلية تتخذ سوى زوجها خليلا، وهو الضمد.\n\nقوله: (عشرا)، أي: عشر ليال، وروي بكسر العين، أي: معاشرة، والمعاشرة: المخالطة، وكذلك التعاشر، والاسم: العشرة. والخليل: الزوج. المعنى: لن يخلص زوج معاشرة امرأة عشر ليال، إلا أن يموت. ذاق الضماد: صفة الخليل.","html":"<p>On “This year no intimate…”: al-Fāʾiq says: “A woman&#x27;s ḍamd <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضمد)</bdi> is her joining together and taking two lovers.” Abū Dhuʾayb said:\n“You wish to join me and Khālid as your lovers—\ncan two swords, woe to you, be joined in one sheath?”\nThe author of the quoted verse is Miqdād ibn Ḥassān al-Zubayrī. It is preceded by:\n“I have found a shared attachment a grievous thing.”\nIn the age of ignorance a woman would take a lover besides her husband; that is ḍamd <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضمد)</bdi>.\nOn “ten” (ʿashran <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عَشْرًا)</bdi>): ten nights. A reading with kasra <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كَسْرَة (ـِ))</bdi> on the ʿayn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عين (ع))</bdi>, ʿishran <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عِشْرًا)</bdi>, is also transmitted, meaning companionship. Muʿāshara <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(معاشرة)</bdi> means association, as does taʿāshur <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(تعاشر)</bdi>; the corresponding noun is ʿishra <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عِشْرة)</bdi>. Khalīl <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(خليل)</bdi> means husband. The meaning is that a husband will not secure exclusive companionship with a woman for ten nights unless he dies. “Who has tasted a shared attachment” describes the intimate. [The compressed verse and its explanation remain difficult; the alternatives are retained rather than conflated.]</p>","lemma":"لن يخلص العام"}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"102:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ أَلْهَىٰكُمُ ٱلتَّكَاثُرُ","en":"Rivalry in worldly increase distracteth you"},{"key":"102:2","number":2,"ar":"حَتَّىٰ زُرْتُمُ ٱلْمَقَابِرَ","en":"Until ye come to the graves"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ أَلْهَىٰكُمُ ٱلتَّكَاثُرُ ﴿1﴾ حَتَّىٰ زُرْتُمُ ٱلْمَقَابِرَ ﴿2﴾","quran_en":"1. Rivalry in worldly increase distracteth you 2. Until ye come to the graves"},{"id":"K102.03","q":"102:3–5","s":102,"surah":"al-Takāthur","surah_ar":"التكاثر","title":"al-Takāthur · Repeated warning","cairo":"K IV:791–792 · T XVI:558–564","source_page_first":791,"source_page_last":792,"chunk":"passages-102","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Repeated warning","html":"<p>Kallā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كلّا)</bdi>, “no indeed,” rebukes and calls attention to the fact that someone concerned for his own welfare ought not to make this world his entire concern while neglecting his religion. “You will come to know” warns them so that they may fear and awaken from heedlessness. Repetition emphasizes the rebuke and warning directed at them. Thumma, “again/then,” indicates that the second warning is more emphatic and severe than the first, as when you tell someone you are advising, “I tell you, and then I tell you again: do not do it.”\nThe meaning is: you will know the error of your present course when you see before you the terror of meeting God. This admonition is counsel and mercy for you. He then repeats the admonition and says, “If only you knew,” with the consequence left unstated: if you knew what lies before you with knowledge of something certain—as you know those matters of which you are assured and to whose knowledge you have devoted your attention—you would do something beyond description or comprehension. But you are astray and ignorant.</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K102.03"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Kallā, stopping points, and the two senses (560–561)","html":"<p>On “kallā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كلّا)</bdi> rebukes and calls attention”: it rejects the preceding discourse and calls attention to what the following discourse indicates. He thus takes both its meanings into consideration. The Imām [Fakhr al-Dīn al-Rāzī] says: “Kallā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كلّا)</bdi> is connected with what precedes by rejection and denial: the matter is not as these people imagine, that true happiness lies in abundant numbers, wealth, and children. It is connected with what follows in the sense, ‘Truly, you will come to know’—but at a time when the transgressor becomes repentant, the unbeliever becomes a Muslim, and the covetous person becomes an ascetic.” The author&#x27;s words intimate both meanings.\nAl-Kawāshī says: “The stop at ‘the graves’ is complete if kallā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كلّا)</bdi> calls attention. If it rebukes, the stop is at kallā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كلّا)</bdi>.”\nIf you ask whether the author&#x27;s position entails using a shared expression simultaneously in both its different meanings, I answer: it does not. The intention is that, if one begins with kallā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كلّا)</bdi>, a new statement begins there. An implied question is supplied: what is the recompense of these heedless people, and what is said of them? The answer is: truly, they will know the outcome of their state when they see the blazing Fire. The discourse thus conveys rebuke by its meaning.\nIf one stops at kallā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كلّا)</bdi>, the implied question occurs after it: what will be done to these rejected people who have been rebuked? The answer is: they will know what will be done to them when they see the blazing Fire. The discourse thus entails calling attention by its meaning. The author of al-Murshid says: “‘Until you visited the graves’ is a complete stop. Begin with kallā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كلّا)</bdi> in the sense of threat and warning.”\nOn “if you knew what lies before you”: it is said that ‘knowledge’ here means knowing the thing itself, not knowing it by its description.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T102.03"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"كلا ردع وتنبيه على أنه لا ينبغي الناظر لنفسه أن تكون الدنيا جميع همه ولا يهتم بدينه سوف تعلمون إنذار ليخافوا فينتبهوا عن غفلتهم. والتكرير: تأكيد للردع والإنذار عليهم. وثم دلالة على أن الإنذار الثاني أبلغ من الأول وأشد، كما تقول للمنصوح: أقول لك ثم أقول لك: لا تفعل. والمعنى:\nسوف تعلمون الخطأ فيما أنتم عليه إذا عاينتم ما قدامكم من هول لقاء الله، وإن هذا التنبيه نصيحة لكم ورحمة عليكم. ثم كرر التنبيه أيضا وقال لو تعلمون محذوف الجواب، يعنى:\nلو تعلمون ما بين أيديكم علم الأمر اليقين، أى: كعلمكم ما تستيقنونه من الأمور التي وكلتم بعلمها هممكم: لفعلتم ما لا يوصف ولا يكتنه، ولكنكم ضلال جهلة،\nقوله: ({كلا}: ردع وتنبيه)، أي: رد للكلام السابق، وتنبيه على ما دل عليه الكلام التالي، فاعتبر في {كلا} كلا مفهوميه، قال الإمام: \"كلا: متصل بما قبله على وجه الرد والتكذيب، أي: ليس الأمر كما يتوهمه هؤلاء من أن السعادة الحقيقية بكثرة العدد والأموال\nوالأولاد، ومتصل بما بعده على معنى: حقا سوف تعلمون، لكن حين يصير الفاسق تائبا، والكافر مسلما، والحريص زاهدا\". وفي كلام المصنف إشعار بهذين المعنيين.\nالكواشي: \"الوقف على {المقابر}: تام، إن جعل {كلا} تنبيها، وإن جعل ردعا، الوقف على {كلا} \".\nفإن قلت: على ما ذهب إليه المصنف، يلزم استعمال اللفظ المشترك في كلا معنييه المخالف. قلت: ليس كذلك؛ إذ المراد أنه إذا ابتدئ بها وقع الاستئناف عندها، فيقدر السؤال: فما جزاء هؤلاء الغفلة، وما يقال في حقهم؟ فيجاب: حقا سيعلمون مآل حالهم حين يرون الجحيم، ففي الكلام ردع من حيث المعنى. وإذا وقف عليها يقع السؤال بعدها، أي: فما يفعل بهؤلاء المطرودين الذين ارتدعوا؟ فيقال: سوف يعلمون ما يفعل بهم حين يرون الجحيم؛ فالكلام مستلزم للتنبيه من حيث المعنى. قال صاحب \"المرشد\": \"حتى زرتم المقابر: وقف تام، وتبتدئ {كلا} في معنى التهديد والوعيد\".\nقوله: (يعني: لو تعلمون ما بين أيديكم)، قيل: المراد بالعلم هاهنا: هو علم الشيء في نفسه، لا علمه على صفته.","layers":[{"id":"K102.03","heading":"K102.03 — Repeated warning","text":"Kallā, “no indeed,” rebukes and calls attention to the fact that someone concerned for his own welfare ought not to make this world his entire concern while neglecting his religion. “You will come to know” warns them so that they may fear and awaken from heedlessness. Repetition emphasizes the rebuke and warning directed at them. Thumma, “again/then,” indicates that the second warning is more emphatic and severe than the first, as when you tell someone you are advising, “I tell you, and then I tell you again: do not do it.”\nThe meaning is: you will know the error of your present course when you see before you the terror of meeting God. This admonition is counsel and mercy for you. He then repeats the admonition and says, “If only you knew,” with the consequence left unstated: if you knew what lies before you with knowledge of something certain—as you know those matters of which you are assured and to whose knowledge you have devoted your attention—you would do something beyond description or comprehension. But you are astray and ignorant.","title":"Repeated warning","author":"kashshaf","surah":102,"ar":"كلا ردع وتنبيه على أنه لا ينبغي الناظر لنفسه أن تكون الدنيا جميع همه ولا يهتم بدينه سوف تعلمون إنذار ليخافوا فينتبهوا عن غفلتهم. والتكرير: تأكيد للردع والإنذار عليهم. وثم دلالة على أن الإنذار الثاني أبلغ من الأول وأشد، كما تقول للمنصوح: أقول لك ثم أقول لك: لا تفعل. والمعنى:\n\nسوف تعلمون الخطأ فيما أنتم عليه إذا عاينتم ما قدامكم من هول لقاء الله، وإن هذا التنبيه نصيحة لكم ورحمة عليكم. ثم كرر التنبيه أيضا وقال لو تعلمون محذوف الجواب، يعنى:\n\nلو تعلمون ما بين أيديكم علم الأمر اليقين، أى: كعلمكم ما تستيقنونه من الأمور التي وكلتم بعلمها هممكم: لفعلتم ما لا يوصف ولا يكتنه، ولكنكم ضلال جهلة،","html":"<p>Kallā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كلّا)</bdi>, “no indeed,” rebukes and calls attention to the fact that someone concerned for his own welfare ought not to make this world his entire concern while neglecting his religion. “You will come to know” warns them so that they may fear and awaken from heedlessness. Repetition emphasizes the rebuke and warning directed at them. Thumma, “again/then,” indicates that the second warning is more emphatic and severe than the first, as when you tell someone you are advising, “I tell you, and then I tell you again: do not do it.”\nThe meaning is: you will know the error of your present course when you see before you the terror of meeting God. This admonition is counsel and mercy for you. He then repeats the admonition and says, “If only you knew,” with the consequence left unstated: if you knew what lies before you with knowledge of something certain—as you know those matters of which you are assured and to whose knowledge you have devoted your attention—you would do something beyond description or comprehension. But you are astray and ignorant.</p>","lemma":""},{"id":"T102.03","heading":"T102.03 — Kallā, stopping points, and the two senses (560–561)","text":"On “kallā rebukes and calls attention”: it rejects the preceding discourse and calls attention to what the following discourse indicates. He thus takes both its meanings into consideration. The Imām [Fakhr al-Dīn al-Rāzī] says: “Kallā is connected with what precedes by rejection and denial: the matter is not as these people imagine, that true happiness lies in abundant numbers, wealth, and children. It is connected with what follows in the sense, ‘Truly, you will come to know’—but at a time when the transgressor becomes repentant, the unbeliever becomes a Muslim, and the covetous person becomes an ascetic.” The author's words intimate both meanings.\nAl-Kawāshī says: “The stop at ‘the graves’ is complete if kallā calls attention. If it rebukes, the stop is at kallā.”\nIf you ask whether the author's position entails using a shared expression simultaneously in both its different meanings, I answer: it does not. The intention is that, if one begins with kallā, a new statement begins there. An implied question is supplied: what is the recompense of these heedless people, and what is said of them? The answer is: truly, they will know the outcome of their state when they see the blazing Fire. The discourse thus conveys rebuke by its meaning.\nIf one stops at kallā, the implied question occurs after it: what will be done to these rejected people who have been rebuked? The answer is: they will know what will be done to them when they see the blazing Fire. The discourse thus entails calling attention by its meaning. The author of al-Murshid says: “‘Until you visited the graves’ is a complete stop. Begin with kallā in the sense of threat and warning.”\nOn “if you knew what lies before you”: it is said that ‘knowledge’ here means knowing the thing itself, not knowing it by its description.","title":"Kallā, stopping points, and the two senses (560–561)","author":"tibi","on":["K102.03"],"anchor":"K102.03","surah":102,"ar":"قوله: ({كلا}: ردع وتنبيه)، أي: رد للكلام السابق، وتنبيه على ما دل عليه الكلام التالي، فاعتبر في {كلا} كلا مفهوميه، قال الإمام: \"كلا: متصل بما قبله على وجه الرد والتكذيب، أي: ليس الأمر كما يتوهمه هؤلاء من أن السعادة الحقيقية بكثرة العدد والأموال\n\nوالأولاد، ومتصل بما بعده على معنى: حقا سوف تعلمون، لكن حين يصير الفاسق تائبا، والكافر مسلما، والحريص زاهدا\". وفي كلام المصنف إشعار بهذين المعنيين.\n\nالكواشي: \"الوقف على {المقابر}: تام، إن جعل {كلا} تنبيها، وإن جعل ردعا، الوقف على {كلا} \".\n\nفإن قلت: على ما ذهب إليه المصنف، يلزم استعمال اللفظ المشترك في كلا معنييه المخالف. قلت: ليس كذلك؛ إذ المراد أنه إذا ابتدئ بها وقع الاستئناف عندها، فيقدر السؤال: فما جزاء هؤلاء الغفلة، وما يقال في حقهم؟ فيجاب: حقا سيعلمون مآل حالهم حين يرون الجحيم، ففي الكلام ردع من حيث المعنى. وإذا وقف عليها يقع السؤال بعدها، أي: فما يفعل بهؤلاء المطرودين الذين ارتدعوا؟ فيقال: سوف يعلمون ما يفعل بهم حين يرون الجحيم؛ فالكلام مستلزم للتنبيه من حيث المعنى. قال صاحب \"المرشد\": \"حتى زرتم المقابر: وقف تام، وتبتدئ {كلا} في معنى التهديد والوعيد\".\n\nقوله: (يعني: لو تعلمون ما بين أيديكم)، قيل: المراد بالعلم هاهنا: هو علم الشيء في نفسه، لا علمه على صفته.","html":"<p>On “kallā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كلّا)</bdi> rebukes and calls attention”: it rejects the preceding discourse and calls attention to what the following discourse indicates. He thus takes both its meanings into consideration. The Imām [Fakhr al-Dīn al-Rāzī] says: “Kallā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كلّا)</bdi> is connected with what precedes by rejection and denial: the matter is not as these people imagine, that true happiness lies in abundant numbers, wealth, and children. It is connected with what follows in the sense, ‘Truly, you will come to know’—but at a time when the transgressor becomes repentant, the unbeliever becomes a Muslim, and the covetous person becomes an ascetic.” The author&#x27;s words intimate both meanings.\nAl-Kawāshī says: “The stop at ‘the graves’ is complete if kallā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كلّا)</bdi> calls attention. If it rebukes, the stop is at kallā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كلّا)</bdi>.”\nIf you ask whether the author&#x27;s position entails using a shared expression simultaneously in both its different meanings, I answer: it does not. The intention is that, if one begins with kallā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كلّا)</bdi>, a new statement begins there. An implied question is supplied: what is the recompense of these heedless people, and what is said of them? The answer is: truly, they will know the outcome of their state when they see the blazing Fire. The discourse thus conveys rebuke by its meaning.\nIf one stops at kallā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كلّا)</bdi>, the implied question occurs after it: what will be done to these rejected people who have been rebuked? The answer is: they will know what will be done to them when they see the blazing Fire. The discourse thus entails calling attention by its meaning. The author of al-Murshid says: “‘Until you visited the graves’ is a complete stop. Begin with kallā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كلّا)</bdi> in the sense of threat and warning.”\nOn “if you knew what lies before you”: it is said that ‘knowledge’ here means knowing the thing itself, not knowing it by its description.</p>","lemma":"{كلا"}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"102:3","number":3,"ar":"كَلَّا سَوْفَ تَعْلَمُونَ","en":"Nay, but ye will come to know"},{"key":"102:4","number":4,"ar":"ثُمَّ كَلَّا سَوْفَ تَعْلَمُونَ","en":"Nay, but ye will come to know"},{"key":"102:5","number":5,"ar":"كَلَّا لَوْ تَعْلَمُونَ عِلْمَ ٱلْيَقِينِ","en":"Nay, would that ye knew (now) with a sure knowledge"}],"quran_ar":"كَلَّا سَوْفَ تَعْلَمُونَ ﴿3﴾ ثُمَّ كَلَّا سَوْفَ تَعْلَمُونَ ﴿4﴾ كَلَّا لَوْ تَعْلَمُونَ عِلْمَ ٱلْيَقِينِ ﴿5﴾","quran_en":"3. Nay, but ye will come to know 4. Nay, but ye will come to know 5. Nay, would that ye knew (now) with a sure knowledge"},{"id":"K102.04","q":"102:6–7","s":102,"surah":"al-Takāthur","surah_ar":"التكاثر","title":"al-Takāthur · Seeing the Fire; readings; certainty","cairo":"K IV:791–792 · T XVI:558–564","source_page_first":791,"source_page_last":792,"chunk":"passages-102","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Seeing the Fire; readings; certainty","html":"<p>He then says, “You will surely see the blazing Fire,” explaining what He had warned them against and threatened them with. The magnifying effect of clarifying something after leaving it unspecified has already been discussed. This is the response to an omitted oath. The oath emphasizes the threat and establishes that what they are threatened with admits no doubt. He repeats it, coordinating it with thumma, to intensify the threat and increase the terror.\nLa-tarʾunna <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لَتَرَؤُنَّ)</bdi> is also read, with a hamza <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(همزة (ء))</bdi> [in place of the wāw <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(واو (و))</bdi>]; it is considered objectionable. If you ask why, when changing a wāw <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(واو (و))</bdi> bearing ḍamma <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضَمَّة (ـُ))</bdi> into hamza <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(همزة (ء))</bdi> is a regular analogy, I answer: that applies to a wāw <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(واو (و))</bdi> whose ḍamma <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضَمَّة (ـُ))</bdi> is permanent. Here it is incidental, introduced to avoid the meeting of two vowelless letters. La-turawunna <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لَتُرَوُنَّ)</bdi> and la-turawunnahā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لَتُرَوُنَّها)</bdi> are also read as passives: “you will surely be made to see,” and “you will surely be made to see it.”\n“With the very certainty of sight” (ʿayna al-yaqīn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عين اليقين)</bdi>): a seeing that is certainty itself and its pure essence. Seeing may mean knowledge and visual perception.</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K102.04"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The hamza reading and the canonical readers (562)","html":"<p>On “that applies to a wāw <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(واو (و))</bdi> whose ḍamma <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضَمَّة (ـُ))</bdi> is permanent”: al-Zajjāj says: “The reading is la-tarawunna <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لَتَرَوُنَّ)</bdi>, with ḍamma <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضَمَّة (ـُ))</bdi> on the wāw <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(واو (و))</bdi> and without hamza <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(همزة (ء))</bdi>. The wāw <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(واو (و))</bdi> receives ḍamma <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضَمَّة (ـُ))</bdi> because it and the nūn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نون (ن))</bdi> are both vowelless. Some have pronounced it with hamza <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(همزة (ء))</bdi>, but the grammarians dislike this because its ḍamma <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضَمَّة (ـُ))</bdi> is not permanent: it was given a vowel to avoid two vowelless letters meeting. They change a wāw <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(واو (و))</bdi> with permanent ḍamma <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضَمَّة (ـُ))</bdi> into hamza <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(همزة (ء))</bdi>, as in adʾur <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(أدؤر)</bdi>, the plural of dār <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(دار)</bdi>, ‘house’; adwur is also permissible.”\nOn “la-turawunna <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لَتُرَوُنَّ)</bdi> is also read”: Ibn ʿĀmir and al-Kisāʾī read with ḍamma <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضَمَّة (ـُ))</bdi> on the tāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(تاء (ت))</bdi>; the others with fatḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فَتْحَة (ـَ))</bdi>. Among the seven readers there is no disagreement that la-tarawunnahā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لَتَرَوُنَّها)</bdi> has fatḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فَتْحَة (ـَ))</bdi> on the tāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(تاء (ت))</bdi>.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T102.04"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Knowledge, sight, and the degrees of certainty (562–563)","html":"<p>On “‘the very certainty of sight’: a seeing that is certainty itself”: it is said that ʿayna al-yaqīn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عين اليقين)</bdi> is accusative as a verbal-noun expression. ʿAyn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عين (ع))</bdi> here means the thing itself, as when you say, “Zayd himself came”—nafsuhu or ʿaynuhu <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عينه)</bdi>. The correct position is that seeing here means visual perception rather than knowledge.\nI say: this is what he intended by “seeing may mean knowledge and visual perception,” treating the coordination as explanatory. The Qāḍī says: “‘ʿAyn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عين (ع))</bdi> al-yaqīn’ means a seeing that is certainty itself, for knowledge through direct witnessing is the highest degree of certainty.”\nOur shaykh, Shaykh al-Islām—may his inner being be sanctified—says in al-ʿAwārif: “ʿIlm al-yaqīn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(علم اليقين)</bdi>, knowledge of certainty, is reached through reflection and inference. ʿAyn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عين (ع))</bdi> al-yaqīn, the eye of certainty, is reached through unveilings and gifts. Ḥaqq al-yaqīn, the reality of certainty, comes through truly separating oneself from the stain of clay when the herald of union arrives.” Al-Junayd says: “The reality of certainty is that the servant becomes fully established in it: he witnesses unseen things just as he witnesses visible things, with direct vision.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T102.05"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"ثم قال لترون الجحيم فبين لهم ما أنذرهم منه وأوعدهم به، وقد مر ما في إيضاح الشيء بعد إبهامه من تفخيمه وتعظيمه، وهو جواب قسم محذوف، والقسم لتوكيد الوعيد، وأن ما أوعدوا به مالا مدخل فيه للريب، وكرره معطوفا بثم تغليظا في التهديد وزيادة في التهويل. وقرئ: لترؤن بالهمز، وهي مستكرهة. فإن قلت: لم استكرهت والواو المضمومة قبلها همزة قياس مطرد؟ قلت:\nذاك في الواو التي ضمتها لازمة، وهذه عارضة لالتقاء الساكنين. وقرئ: لترون، ولترونها: على البناء للمفعول عين اليقين أى الرؤية التي هي نفس اليقين وخالصته. ويجوز أن يراد بالرؤية: \nالعلم والإبصار\nقوله: (ذاك في الواو التي ضمتها لازمة)، قال الزجاج: \"القراءة: {لترون}، بضم الواو غير مهموز، فضمت الواو لسكونها وسكون النون، وقد همزها بعضهم، والنحويون يكرهونها لأن ضمتها غير لازمة، لأنها حركت لالتقاء الساكنين، ويهمزون الواو التي ضمتها لازمة، نحو: أدؤر، جمع دار، ويجوز: أدور أيضا\".\nقوله: (وقرئ: \"لترون\")، ابن عامر والكسائي: بضم التاء، والباقون: بفتحها. ولا خلاف في السبعة في قوله: {لترونها} بفتح التاء.\nقوله: ({عين اليقين}: أي: الرؤية التي هي نفس اليقين)، قيل: أراد أن {عين اليقين} نصب على المصدر، والعين هاهنا بمعنى نفس الشيء، كقولك: جاء زيد نفسه وعينه. والصواب أن الرؤية هاهنا بمعنى الإبصار لا العلم.\nوقلت: هذا هو الذي أراده بقوله: \"ويجوز أن يراد بالرؤية العلم والإبصار\"، على العطف التفسيري. وقال القاضي: \"عين اليقين: الرؤية التي هي نفس اليقين؛ فإن علم المشاهدة أعلى مراتب اليقين\".\nوقال شيخنا شيخ الإسلام قدس سره في \"العوارف\": \"علم اليقين ما كان من طريق النظر والاستدلال، وعين اليقين ما كان من طريق الكشوف والنوال، وحق اليقين ما كان بتحقيق الانفصال عن لوث الصلصال، بورود رائد الوصال. وقال الجنيد: حق اليقين ما يتحقق العبد بذلك، وهو أن يشاهد الغيوب كما يشاهد المرئيات مشاهدة عيان\".","layers":[{"id":"K102.04","heading":"K102.04 — Seeing the Fire; readings; certainty","text":"He then says, “You will surely see the blazing Fire,” explaining what He had warned them against and threatened them with. The magnifying effect of clarifying something after leaving it unspecified has already been discussed. This is the response to an omitted oath. The oath emphasizes the threat and establishes that what they are threatened with admits no doubt. He repeats it, coordinating it with thumma, to intensify the threat and increase the terror.\nLa-tarʾunna is also read, with a hamza [in place of the wāw]; it is considered objectionable. If you ask why, when changing a wāw bearing ḍamma into hamza is a regular analogy, I answer: that applies to a wāw whose ḍamma is permanent. Here it is incidental, introduced to avoid the meeting of two vowelless letters. La-turawunna and la-turawunnahā are also read as passives: “you will surely be made to see,” and “you will surely be made to see it.”\n“With the very certainty of sight” (ʿayna al-yaqīn): a seeing that is certainty itself and its pure essence. Seeing may mean knowledge and visual perception.","title":"Seeing the Fire; readings; certainty","author":"kashshaf","surah":102,"ar":"ثم قال لترون الجحيم فبين لهم ما أنذرهم منه وأوعدهم به، وقد مر ما في إيضاح الشيء بعد إبهامه من تفخيمه وتعظيمه، وهو جواب قسم محذوف، والقسم لتوكيد الوعيد، وأن ما أوعدوا به مالا مدخل فيه للريب، وكرره معطوفا بثم تغليظا في التهديد وزيادة في التهويل. وقرئ: لترؤن بالهمز، وهي مستكرهة. فإن قلت: لم استكرهت والواو المضمومة قبلها همزة قياس مطرد؟ قلت:\n\nذاك في الواو التي ضمتها لازمة، وهذه عارضة لالتقاء الساكنين. وقرئ: لترون، ولترونها: على البناء للمفعول عين اليقين أى الرؤية التي هي نفس اليقين وخالصته. ويجوز أن يراد بالرؤية: \n\nالعلم والإبصار","html":"<p>He then says, “You will surely see the blazing Fire,” explaining what He had warned them against and threatened them with. The magnifying effect of clarifying something after leaving it unspecified has already been discussed. This is the response to an omitted oath. The oath emphasizes the threat and establishes that what they are threatened with admits no doubt. He repeats it, coordinating it with thumma, to intensify the threat and increase the terror.\nLa-tarʾunna <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لَتَرَؤُنَّ)</bdi> is also read, with a hamza <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(همزة (ء))</bdi> [in place of the wāw <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(واو (و))</bdi>]; it is considered objectionable. If you ask why, when changing a wāw <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(واو (و))</bdi> bearing ḍamma <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضَمَّة (ـُ))</bdi> into hamza <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(همزة (ء))</bdi> is a regular analogy, I answer: that applies to a wāw <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(واو (و))</bdi> whose ḍamma <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضَمَّة (ـُ))</bdi> is permanent. Here it is incidental, introduced to avoid the meeting of two vowelless letters. La-turawunna <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لَتُرَوُنَّ)</bdi> and la-turawunnahā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لَتُرَوُنَّها)</bdi> are also read as passives: “you will surely be made to see,” and “you will surely be made to see it.”\n“With the very certainty of sight” (ʿayna al-yaqīn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عين اليقين)</bdi>): a seeing that is certainty itself and its pure essence. Seeing may mean knowledge and visual perception.</p>","lemma":""},{"id":"T102.04","heading":"T102.04 — The hamza reading and the canonical readers (562)","text":"On “that applies to a wāw whose ḍamma is permanent”: al-Zajjāj says: “The reading is la-tarawunna, with ḍamma on the wāw and without hamza. The wāw receives ḍamma because it and the nūn are both vowelless. Some have pronounced it with hamza, but the grammarians dislike this because its ḍamma is not permanent: it was given a vowel to avoid two vowelless letters meeting. They change a wāw with permanent ḍamma into hamza, as in adʾur, the plural of dār, ‘house’; adwur is also permissible.”\nOn “la-turawunna is also read”: Ibn ʿĀmir and al-Kisāʾī read with ḍamma on the tāʾ; the others with fatḥa. Among the seven readers there is no disagreement that la-tarawunnahā has fatḥa on the tāʾ.","title":"The hamza reading and the canonical readers (562)","author":"tibi","on":["K102.04"],"anchor":"K102.04","surah":102,"ar":"قوله: (ذاك في الواو التي ضمتها لازمة)، قال الزجاج: \"القراءة: {لترون}، بضم الواو غير مهموز، فضمت الواو لسكونها وسكون النون، وقد همزها بعضهم، والنحويون يكرهونها لأن ضمتها غير لازمة، لأنها حركت لالتقاء الساكنين، ويهمزون الواو التي ضمتها لازمة، نحو: أدؤر، جمع دار، ويجوز: أدور أيضا\".\n\nقوله: (وقرئ: \"لترون\")، ابن عامر والكسائي: بضم التاء، والباقون: بفتحها. ولا خلاف في السبعة في قوله: {لترونها} بفتح التاء.","html":"<p>On “that applies to a wāw <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(واو (و))</bdi> whose ḍamma <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضَمَّة (ـُ))</bdi> is permanent”: al-Zajjāj says: “The reading is la-tarawunna <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لَتَرَوُنَّ)</bdi>, with ḍamma <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضَمَّة (ـُ))</bdi> on the wāw <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(واو (و))</bdi> and without hamza <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(همزة (ء))</bdi>. The wāw <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(واو (و))</bdi> receives ḍamma <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضَمَّة (ـُ))</bdi> because it and the nūn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نون (ن))</bdi> are both vowelless. Some have pronounced it with hamza <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(همزة (ء))</bdi>, but the grammarians dislike this because its ḍamma <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضَمَّة (ـُ))</bdi> is not permanent: it was given a vowel to avoid two vowelless letters meeting. They change a wāw <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(واو (و))</bdi> with permanent ḍamma <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضَمَّة (ـُ))</bdi> into hamza <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(همزة (ء))</bdi>, as in adʾur <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(أدؤر)</bdi>, the plural of dār <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(دار)</bdi>, ‘house’; adwur is also permissible.”\nOn “la-turawunna <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لَتُرَوُنَّ)</bdi> is also read”: Ibn ʿĀmir and al-Kisāʾī read with ḍamma <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ضَمَّة (ـُ))</bdi> on the tāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(تاء (ت))</bdi>; the others with fatḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فَتْحَة (ـَ))</bdi>. Among the seven readers there is no disagreement that la-tarawunnahā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لَتَرَوُنَّها)</bdi> has fatḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فَتْحَة (ـَ))</bdi> on the tāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(تاء (ت))</bdi>.</p>","lemma":"ذاك في الواو التي ضمتها لازمة"},{"id":"T102.05","heading":"T102.05 — Knowledge, sight, and the degrees of certainty (562–563)","text":"On “‘the very certainty of sight’: a seeing that is certainty itself”: it is said that ʿayna al-yaqīn is accusative as a verbal-noun expression. ʿAyn here means the thing itself, as when you say, “Zayd himself came”—nafsuhu or ʿaynuhu. The correct position is that seeing here means visual perception rather than knowledge.\nI say: this is what he intended by “seeing may mean knowledge and visual perception,” treating the coordination as explanatory. The Qāḍī says: “‘ʿAyn al-yaqīn’ means a seeing that is certainty itself, for knowledge through direct witnessing is the highest degree of certainty.”\nOur shaykh, Shaykh al-Islām—may his inner being be sanctified—says in al-ʿAwārif: “ʿIlm al-yaqīn, knowledge of certainty, is reached through reflection and inference. ʿAyn al-yaqīn, the eye of certainty, is reached through unveilings and gifts. Ḥaqq al-yaqīn, the reality of certainty, comes through truly separating oneself from the stain of clay when the herald of union arrives.” Al-Junayd says: “The reality of certainty is that the servant becomes fully established in it: he witnesses unseen things just as he witnesses visible things, with direct vision.”","title":"Knowledge, sight, and the degrees of certainty (562–563)","author":"tibi","on":["K102.04"],"anchor":"K102.04","surah":102,"ar":"قوله: ({عين اليقين}: أي: الرؤية التي هي نفس اليقين)، قيل: أراد أن {عين اليقين} نصب على المصدر، والعين هاهنا بمعنى نفس الشيء، كقولك: جاء زيد نفسه وعينه. والصواب أن الرؤية هاهنا بمعنى الإبصار لا العلم.\n\nوقلت: هذا هو الذي أراده بقوله: \"ويجوز أن يراد بالرؤية العلم والإبصار\"، على العطف التفسيري. وقال القاضي: \"عين اليقين: الرؤية التي هي نفس اليقين؛ فإن علم المشاهدة أعلى مراتب اليقين\".\n\nوقال شيخنا شيخ الإسلام قدس سره في \"العوارف\": \"علم اليقين ما كان من طريق النظر والاستدلال، وعين اليقين ما كان من طريق الكشوف والنوال، وحق اليقين ما كان بتحقيق الانفصال عن لوث الصلصال، بورود رائد الوصال. وقال الجنيد: حق اليقين ما يتحقق العبد بذلك، وهو أن يشاهد الغيوب كما يشاهد المرئيات مشاهدة عيان\".","html":"<p>On “‘the very certainty of sight’: a seeing that is certainty itself”: it is said that ʿayna al-yaqīn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عين اليقين)</bdi> is accusative as a verbal-noun expression. ʿAyn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عين (ع))</bdi> here means the thing itself, as when you say, “Zayd himself came”—nafsuhu or ʿaynuhu <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عينه)</bdi>. The correct position is that seeing here means visual perception rather than knowledge.\nI say: this is what he intended by “seeing may mean knowledge and visual perception,” treating the coordination as explanatory. The Qāḍī says: “‘ʿAyn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عين (ع))</bdi> al-yaqīn’ means a seeing that is certainty itself, for knowledge through direct witnessing is the highest degree of certainty.”\nOur shaykh, Shaykh al-Islām—may his inner being be sanctified—says in al-ʿAwārif: “ʿIlm al-yaqīn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(علم اليقين)</bdi>, knowledge of certainty, is reached through reflection and inference. ʿAyn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عين (ع))</bdi> al-yaqīn, the eye of certainty, is reached through unveilings and gifts. Ḥaqq al-yaqīn, the reality of certainty, comes through truly separating oneself from the stain of clay when the herald of union arrives.” Al-Junayd says: “The reality of certainty is that the servant becomes fully established in it: he witnesses unseen things just as he witnesses visible things, with direct vision.”</p>","lemma":"{عين اليقين"}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"102:6","number":6,"ar":"لَتَرَوُنَّ ٱلْجَحِيمَ","en":"For ye will behold hell-fire"},{"key":"102:7","number":7,"ar":"ثُمَّ لَتَرَوُنَّهَا عَيْنَ ٱلْيَقِينِ","en":"Aye, ye will behold it with sure vision"}],"quran_ar":"لَتَرَوُنَّ ٱلْجَحِيمَ ﴿6﴾ ثُمَّ لَتَرَوُنَّهَا عَيْنَ ٱلْيَقِينِ ﴿7﴾","quran_en":"6. For ye will behold hell-fire 7. Aye, ye will behold it with sure vision"},{"id":"K102.05","q":"102:8","s":102,"surah":"al-Takāthur","surah_ar":"التكاثر","title":"al-Takāthur · The enjoyment about which people are questioned","cairo":"K IV:791–792 · T XVI:558–564","source_page_first":791,"source_page_last":792,"chunk":"passages-102","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The enjoyment about which people are questioned","html":"<p>“About the pleasures you enjoyed”: about the amusement and comfortable enjoyment whose pleasures distracted you from religion and its obligations.\nIf you ask what enjoyment a person is questioned and reproached about, since everyone has some enjoyment, I answer: it is the enjoyment of someone who devotes all his concern to exhausting the pleasures of life, living only to eat fine food, wear soft clothes, and pass his time in amusement and entertainment. He pays no heed to knowledge and action and does not undertake their difficulties.\nBut someone who enjoys God&#x27;s blessings and provisions—which He created only for His servants—and draws strength from them to study knowledge and perform good works, while rising to the duty of gratitude, is far removed from this. God&#x27;s Messenger indicated this in the report that he and his Companions ate dates and drank water afterward, and he said: “Praise belongs to God, who fed us, gave us drink, and made us Muslims.”\nGod&#x27;s Messenger is reported to have said: “Whoever recites ‘Rivalry in increase has distracted you,’ God will not call him to account for the enjoyment He granted him in the abode of this world, and he will be given a reward as though he had recited a thousand verses.”</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K102.05"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Who is questioned about enjoyment? (563–564)","html":"<p>On “the enjoyment of someone who devotes all his concern to exhausting pleasures”: the Qāḍī says: “The address, ‘Then that day you will surely be questioned about the pleasures you enjoyed,’ concerns specifically anyone whose worldly life distracted him from his religion, not the believers, because of the contextual indication and the many texts such as ‘Say: Who has forbidden God&#x27;s adornment, which He has brought forth for His servants, and the good things of provision?’ (7:32), and ‘Eat of the good things’ (23:51). It is also said to concern unbelievers specifically; or to be general, since everyone is questioned about his gratitude.”\nI say: the latter is supported by what we have transmitted from Muslim, al-Tirmidhī, and Ibn Mājah, from Abū Hurayra. God&#x27;s Messenger went out and encountered Abū Bakr and ʿUmar. He asked: “What brought you out of your homes?” They said: “Hunger.” He said: “By Him in whose hand is my soul, the same thing brought me out.” They came to the house of a man of the Anṣār, who brought them a cluster containing unripe, dried, and fresh dates, and slaughtered an animal for them. They ate from the sheep and the date-cluster and drank. When they had eaten and drunk their fill, God&#x27;s Messenger said to them: “By Him in whose hand is my soul, you will surely be questioned about this enjoyment on the Day of Resurrection.” The report is abbreviated here.\nAl-Wāḥidī transmits from Muqātil: “He means the unbelievers of Mecca. In this world they lived amid goodness and blessings. On the Day of Resurrection they will be questioned about gratitude for what they enjoyed. They did not thank the Lord of blessings, since they worshipped others and associated partners with Him; then they will be punished. This is al-Ḥasan&#x27;s position.”\nI say: this is supported by the fact that the address from the beginning of the sūrah is to those competing and boasting in abundance, who, as explained above, are unbelievers. Since preoccupation with worldly enjoyment is a characteristic of the heedless, and a believer must avoid base qualities, God&#x27;s Messenger spoke sternly when he said, “You will be questioned about this enjoyment on the Day of Resurrection.” This does not mean that he was interpreting the verse by what he said.\nComplete.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T102.06"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"عن النعيم عن اللهو والتنعم الذي شغلكم الالتذاذ به عن الدين وتكاليفه.\nفإن قلت: ما النعيم الذي يسئل عنه الإنسان ويعاتب عليه؟ فما من أحد إلا وله نعيم؟ قلت:\nهو نعيم من عكف همته على استيفاء اللذات، ولم يعش إلا ليأكل الطيب ويلبس اللين، ويقطع أوقاته باللهو والطرب، لا يعبأ بالعلم والعمل، ولا يحمل نفسه مشاقهما، فأما من تمتع بنعمة الله وأرزاقه التي لم يخلقها إلا لعباده، وتقوى بها على دراسة العلم والقيام بالعمل، وكان ناهضا بالشكر: فهو من ذاك بمعزل، وإليه أشار رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فيما يروى: أنه أكل هو وأصحابه تمرا وشربوا عليه ماء فقال: «الحمد لله الذي أطعمنا وسقانا وجعلنا مسلمين» .\nعن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: «من قرأ ألهاكم التكاثر لم يحاسبه الله بالنعيم الذي أنعم به عليه في دار الدنيا، وأعطى من الأجر كأنما قرأ ألف آية» .\nقوله: (هو نعيم من عكف همته على استيفاء اللذات)، قال القاضي: \"الخطاب بقوله: {ثم لتسئلن يومئذ عن النعيم}، مخصوص بكل من ألهاه دنياه عن دينه، لا بالمؤمنين للقرينة\nوالنصوص الكثيرة، كقوله تعالى: {قل من حرم زينة الله التي أخرج لعباده والطيبات من الرزق} [الأعراف: 32]، وقوله: {كلوا من الطيبات} [المؤمنون: 51]. وقيل: مخصوص بالكفار، وقيل: عام؛ إذ كل يسأل عن شكره\".\nوقلت: ويعضده ما روينا عن مسلم والترمذي وابن ماجه، عن أبي هريرة: خرج رسول الله? ، فإذا هو بأبي بكر وعمر رضي الله عنهما، فقال: ما أخرجكما عن بيتكما؟ قالا: الجوع. قال: وأنا، والذي نفسي بيده، لأخرجني الذي أخرجكما. فجاؤوا بيت أنصاري، فجاءهم بعذق فيه بسر وتمر ورطب وذبح لهم، فأكلوا من الشاة والعذق وشربوا، فلما أن شبعوا ورووا، قال رسول الله? لهما: \"والذي نفسي بيده، لتسألن عن هذا النعيم يوم القيامة\". الحديث مختصر.\nوروى الواحدي عن مقاتل: \"يعني كفار مكة، كانوا في الدنيا في الخير والنعمة، فيسألون يوم القيامو عن شكر ما كانوا فيه ول يشكروا رب النعم، حيث عبدوا غيره وأشركوا به، ثم يعذبون. هذا قول الحسن\".\nوقلت: ويؤيده أن الخطاب من أول السورة مع المتكاثرين والمتباهين وهم كفرة، على ما سبق. ولما كان الاشتغال بنعيم الدنيا من صفات الغافلين، ويجب على المؤمن أن يجتنب عن رذائل الأخلاق، غلظ رسول الله? حيث قال: لتسألن عن هذا النعيم يوم القيامة، لا أنه صلوات الله عليه فسر الآية بما قال.\nتمت","layers":[{"id":"K102.05","heading":"K102.05 — The enjoyment about which people are questioned","text":"“About the pleasures you enjoyed”: about the amusement and comfortable enjoyment whose pleasures distracted you from religion and its obligations.\nIf you ask what enjoyment a person is questioned and reproached about, since everyone has some enjoyment, I answer: it is the enjoyment of someone who devotes all his concern to exhausting the pleasures of life, living only to eat fine food, wear soft clothes, and pass his time in amusement and entertainment. He pays no heed to knowledge and action and does not undertake their difficulties.\nBut someone who enjoys God's blessings and provisions—which He created only for His servants—and draws strength from them to study knowledge and perform good works, while rising to the duty of gratitude, is far removed from this. God's Messenger indicated this in the report that he and his Companions ate dates and drank water afterward, and he said: “Praise belongs to God, who fed us, gave us drink, and made us Muslims.”\nGod's Messenger is reported to have said: “Whoever recites ‘Rivalry in increase has distracted you,’ God will not call him to account for the enjoyment He granted him in the abode of this world, and he will be given a reward as though he had recited a thousand verses.”","title":"The enjoyment about which people are questioned","author":"kashshaf","surah":102,"ar":"عن النعيم عن اللهو والتنعم الذي شغلكم الالتذاذ به عن الدين وتكاليفه.\n\nفإن قلت: ما النعيم الذي يسئل عنه الإنسان ويعاتب عليه؟ فما من أحد إلا وله نعيم؟ قلت:\n\nهو نعيم من عكف همته على استيفاء اللذات، ولم يعش إلا ليأكل الطيب ويلبس اللين، ويقطع أوقاته باللهو والطرب، لا يعبأ بالعلم والعمل، ولا يحمل نفسه مشاقهما، فأما من تمتع بنعمة الله وأرزاقه التي لم يخلقها إلا لعباده، وتقوى بها على دراسة العلم والقيام بالعمل، وكان ناهضا بالشكر: فهو من ذاك بمعزل، وإليه أشار رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فيما يروى: أنه أكل هو وأصحابه تمرا وشربوا عليه ماء فقال: «الحمد لله الذي أطعمنا وسقانا وجعلنا مسلمين» .\n\nعن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: «من قرأ ألهاكم التكاثر لم يحاسبه الله بالنعيم الذي أنعم به عليه في دار الدنيا، وأعطى من الأجر كأنما قرأ ألف آية» .","html":"<p>“About the pleasures you enjoyed”: about the amusement and comfortable enjoyment whose pleasures distracted you from religion and its obligations.\nIf you ask what enjoyment a person is questioned and reproached about, since everyone has some enjoyment, I answer: it is the enjoyment of someone who devotes all his concern to exhausting the pleasures of life, living only to eat fine food, wear soft clothes, and pass his time in amusement and entertainment. He pays no heed to knowledge and action and does not undertake their difficulties.\nBut someone who enjoys God&#x27;s blessings and provisions—which He created only for His servants—and draws strength from them to study knowledge and perform good works, while rising to the duty of gratitude, is far removed from this. God&#x27;s Messenger indicated this in the report that he and his Companions ate dates and drank water afterward, and he said: “Praise belongs to God, who fed us, gave us drink, and made us Muslims.”\nGod&#x27;s Messenger is reported to have said: “Whoever recites ‘Rivalry in increase has distracted you,’ God will not call him to account for the enjoyment He granted him in the abode of this world, and he will be given a reward as though he had recited a thousand verses.”</p>","lemma":""},{"id":"T102.06","heading":"T102.06 — Who is questioned about enjoyment? (563–564)","text":"On “the enjoyment of someone who devotes all his concern to exhausting pleasures”: the Qāḍī says: “The address, ‘Then that day you will surely be questioned about the pleasures you enjoyed,’ concerns specifically anyone whose worldly life distracted him from his religion, not the believers, because of the contextual indication and the many texts such as ‘Say: Who has forbidden God's adornment, which He has brought forth for His servants, and the good things of provision?’ (7:32), and ‘Eat of the good things’ (23:51). It is also said to concern unbelievers specifically; or to be general, since everyone is questioned about his gratitude.”\nI say: the latter is supported by what we have transmitted from Muslim, al-Tirmidhī, and Ibn Mājah, from Abū Hurayra. God's Messenger went out and encountered Abū Bakr and ʿUmar. He asked: “What brought you out of your homes?” They said: “Hunger.” He said: “By Him in whose hand is my soul, the same thing brought me out.” They came to the house of a man of the Anṣār, who brought them a cluster containing unripe, dried, and fresh dates, and slaughtered an animal for them. They ate from the sheep and the date-cluster and drank. When they had eaten and drunk their fill, God's Messenger said to them: “By Him in whose hand is my soul, you will surely be questioned about this enjoyment on the Day of Resurrection.” The report is abbreviated here.\nAl-Wāḥidī transmits from Muqātil: “He means the unbelievers of Mecca. In this world they lived amid goodness and blessings. On the Day of Resurrection they will be questioned about gratitude for what they enjoyed. They did not thank the Lord of blessings, since they worshipped others and associated partners with Him; then they will be punished. This is al-Ḥasan's position.”\nI say: this is supported by the fact that the address from the beginning of the sūrah is to those competing and boasting in abundance, who, as explained above, are unbelievers. Since preoccupation with worldly enjoyment is a characteristic of the heedless, and a believer must avoid base qualities, God's Messenger spoke sternly when he said, “You will be questioned about this enjoyment on the Day of Resurrection.” This does not mean that he was interpreting the verse by what he said.\nComplete.","title":"Who is questioned about enjoyment? (563–564)","author":"tibi","on":["K102.05"],"anchor":"K102.05","surah":102,"ar":"قوله: (هو نعيم من عكف همته على استيفاء اللذات)، قال القاضي: \"الخطاب بقوله: {ثم لتسئلن يومئذ عن النعيم}، مخصوص بكل من ألهاه دنياه عن دينه، لا بالمؤمنين للقرينة\n\nوالنصوص الكثيرة، كقوله تعالى: {قل من حرم زينة الله التي أخرج لعباده والطيبات من الرزق} [الأعراف: 32]، وقوله: {كلوا من الطيبات} [المؤمنون: 51]. وقيل: مخصوص بالكفار، وقيل: عام؛ إذ كل يسأل عن شكره\".\n\nوقلت: ويعضده ما روينا عن مسلم والترمذي وابن ماجه، عن أبي هريرة: خرج رسول الله? ، فإذا هو بأبي بكر وعمر رضي الله عنهما، فقال: ما أخرجكما عن بيتكما؟ قالا: الجوع. قال: وأنا، والذي نفسي بيده، لأخرجني الذي أخرجكما. فجاؤوا بيت أنصاري، فجاءهم بعذق فيه بسر وتمر ورطب وذبح لهم، فأكلوا من الشاة والعذق وشربوا، فلما أن شبعوا ورووا، قال رسول الله? لهما: \"والذي نفسي بيده، لتسألن عن هذا النعيم يوم القيامة\". الحديث مختصر.\n\nوروى الواحدي عن مقاتل: \"يعني كفار مكة، كانوا في الدنيا في الخير والنعمة، فيسألون يوم القيامو عن شكر ما كانوا فيه ول يشكروا رب النعم، حيث عبدوا غيره وأشركوا به، ثم يعذبون. هذا قول الحسن\".\n\nوقلت: ويؤيده أن الخطاب من أول السورة مع المتكاثرين والمتباهين وهم كفرة، على ما سبق. ولما كان الاشتغال بنعيم الدنيا من صفات الغافلين، ويجب على المؤمن أن يجتنب عن رذائل الأخلاق، غلظ رسول الله? حيث قال: لتسألن عن هذا النعيم يوم القيامة، لا أنه صلوات الله عليه فسر الآية بما قال.\n\nتمت","html":"<p>On “the enjoyment of someone who devotes all his concern to exhausting pleasures”: the Qāḍī says: “The address, ‘Then that day you will surely be questioned about the pleasures you enjoyed,’ concerns specifically anyone whose worldly life distracted him from his religion, not the believers, because of the contextual indication and the many texts such as ‘Say: Who has forbidden God&#x27;s adornment, which He has brought forth for His servants, and the good things of provision?’ (7:32), and ‘Eat of the good things’ (23:51). It is also said to concern unbelievers specifically; or to be general, since everyone is questioned about his gratitude.”\nI say: the latter is supported by what we have transmitted from Muslim, al-Tirmidhī, and Ibn Mājah, from Abū Hurayra. God&#x27;s Messenger went out and encountered Abū Bakr and ʿUmar. He asked: “What brought you out of your homes?” They said: “Hunger.” He said: “By Him in whose hand is my soul, the same thing brought me out.” They came to the house of a man of the Anṣār, who brought them a cluster containing unripe, dried, and fresh dates, and slaughtered an animal for them. They ate from the sheep and the date-cluster and drank. When they had eaten and drunk their fill, God&#x27;s Messenger said to them: “By Him in whose hand is my soul, you will surely be questioned about this enjoyment on the Day of Resurrection.” The report is abbreviated here.\nAl-Wāḥidī transmits from Muqātil: “He means the unbelievers of Mecca. In this world they lived amid goodness and blessings. On the Day of Resurrection they will be questioned about gratitude for what they enjoyed. They did not thank the Lord of blessings, since they worshipped others and associated partners with Him; then they will be punished. This is al-Ḥasan&#x27;s position.”\nI say: this is supported by the fact that the address from the beginning of the sūrah is to those competing and boasting in abundance, who, as explained above, are unbelievers. Since preoccupation with worldly enjoyment is a characteristic of the heedless, and a believer must avoid base qualities, God&#x27;s Messenger spoke sternly when he said, “You will be questioned about this enjoyment on the Day of Resurrection.” This does not mean that he was interpreting the verse by what he said.\nComplete.</p>","lemma":"هو نعيم من عكف همته على استيفاء اللذات"}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"102:8","number":8,"ar":"ثُمَّ لَتُسْـَٔلُنَّ يَوْمَئِذٍ عَنِ ٱلنَّعِيمِ","en":"Then, on that day, ye will be asked concerning pleasure"}],"quran_ar":"ثُمَّ لَتُسْـَٔلُنَّ يَوْمَئِذٍ عَنِ ٱلنَّعِيمِ ﴿8﴾","quran_en":"8. Then, on that day, ye will be asked concerning pleasure"}];
